UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 38, Lesson 2: Making a healthy habits plan', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART idea: Specific (what exactly), Measurable (how often or how long), Achievable (realistic), Relevant (it matters to you) and Time-bound (by when). A plan can cover food, water, activity, sleep and screen time. Plans are reviewed and changed, and families can help each other.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite ''I will be healthier'' as a SMART goal about drinking water.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific, measurable, achievable and time-bound.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a plan has to be perfect on day one. Plans are reviewed and changed when something is not working.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a four-week healthy habits plan with one SMART goal each for food, water, activity and sleep. Add a tracking chart, one way your family can help and one review date. Do not compare your plan with other people''s.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART idea: Specific (what exactly), Measurable (how often or how long), Achievable (realistic), Relevant (it matters to you) and Time-bound (by when). A plan can cover food, water, activity, sleep and screen time. Plans are reviewed and changed, and families can help each other.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a four-week healthy habits plan with one SMART goal each for food, water, activity and sleep. Add a tracking chart, one way your family can help and one review date. Do not compare your plan with other people''s. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using a habits plan on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one.. Correction: Sample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one. Plans are reviewed and changed when something is not working. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a habits plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a habits plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about a habits plan with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a habits plan. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for a habits plan. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Sample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific","Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson"],"question":"Rewrite ''I will be healthier'' as a SMART goal about drinking water. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for a habits plan; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one","A healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART idea: Specific (what exactly), Measurable (how","Sample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific","Careful use of a SMART goal and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with a habits plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named a SMART goal and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a SMART goal and explain why it matters only, without linking it to a habits plan","Apply a habits plan to new material and name the a SMART goal and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one"],"question":"Which response best applies a habits plan with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses safe, healthy or respectful choices appropriately for this lesson on a habits plan. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one","Sample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific"],"question":"Which response best uses safe, healthy or respectful choices for a habits plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one","Name a body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy and say how it helps with a habits plan","Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about a habits plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (A healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Choose the fastest option even when it ignores safety","Use a put-down and call it honesty","Example four-week plan. Food: add one vegetable at dinner on 5 nights. Water: refill the bottle at lunch every school","Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a habits plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue","Check safety and fairness, include others, and explain the healthier choice","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete"],"question":"Which action keeps safe, healthy or respectful choices accurate for a habits plan?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a SMART goal and explain why it matters only, with no linked example","Make a four-week healthy habits plan with one SMART goal each for food, water, activity and sleep","Retype the worked example with no new example","Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one"],"question":"Which independent response best applies a habits plan to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about a habits plan with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a habits plan on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using a habits plan on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for a habits plan. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with a habits plan, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one.. Correction: Sample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w38-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a habits plan.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a habits plan with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a SMART goal and explain why it matters.","I can practise tracking independently on a new prompt.","I can notice reviewing a plan needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART idea: Specific (what exactly), Measurable (how often or how long), Achievable (realistic), Relevant (it matters to you) and Time-bound (by when).","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART idea: Specific (what exactly), Measurable (how","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a habits plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample four-week plan. Food: add one vegetable at dinner on 5 nights. Water: refill the bottle at lunch every school day. Activity: a 20-minute walk 4 times a week. Sleep: lights out at 8:30 on school nights. Review: tick a chart each week. Family help: Dad walks with me.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite ''I will be healthier'' as a SMART goal about drinking water.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific, measurable, achievable and time-bound.","explain":"A healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART idea: Specific (what exactly), Measurable (how often or how long), Achievable (realistic), Relevant (it matters to you) and Time-bound (by when). A plan can cover food, water, activity, sleep and screen time. Plans are reviewed and changed, and families can help each other.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one. Plans are reviewed and changed when something is not working. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A vague and a SMART goal.","caption":"Make goals measurable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Vague</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">be healthier</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">SMART</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">6 cups for 2 weeks</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example four-week plan. Food: add one vegetable at dinner on 5 nights. Water: refill the bottle at lunch every school da Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example four-week plan. Food: add one vegetable at dinner on 5 nights. Water: refill the bottle at lunch every school da Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a habits plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample four-week plan. Food: add one vegetable at dinner on 5 nights. Water: refill the bottle at lunch every school day. Activity: a 20-minute walk 4 times a week. Sleep: lights out at 8:30 on school nights. Review: tick a chart each week. Family help: Dad walks with me. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"A habits plan.","caption":"Review and adjust.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">food, water, activity, sleep</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Review</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tick a chart weekly</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison","A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a habits plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving a plan has to be perfect on day one. Plans are reviewed and changed when something is not working. Here is a more helpful example: Example four-week plan. Food: add one vegetable at dinner on 5 nights. Water: refill the bottle at lunch every school day. Activity: a 20-minute walk 4 times a week. Sleep: lights out at 8:30 on school nights. Review: tick a chart each week. Family help: Dad walks with me.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''I will drink 6 cups of water each school day for the next two weeks, tracked on a chart.'' It is specific, measurable, achievable and time-bound.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a plan has to be perfect on day one. Plans are reviewed and changed when something is not working. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example.","options":["A guess with no reason","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect","Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about a habits plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA healthy habits plan brings several habits together using the SMART idea: Specific (what exactly), Measurable (how often or how long), Achievable (realistic), Relevant (it matters to you) and Time-bound (by when). A plan can cover food, water, activity, sleep and screen time. Plans are reviewed and changed, and families can help each other. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 38, Lesson 2: Making a healthy habits plan","unit_number":8,"unit_title":"Healthy habits","week":38,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample four-week plan. Food: add one vegetable at dinner on 5 nights. Water: refill the bottle at lunch every school day. Activity: a 20-minute walk 4 times a week. Sleep: lights out at 8:30 on school nights. Review: tick a chart each week. Family help: Dad walks with me.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A vague and a SMART goal.","caption":"Make goals measurable.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Vague</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">be healthier</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">SMART</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">6 cups for 2 weeks</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"A habits plan.","caption":"Review and adjust.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Plan</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">food, water, activity, sleep</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Review</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">tick a chart weekly</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-pdhpe-y3-w38-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
